{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-14003",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}-60x & =15x \\\\ 5{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}-75x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5x.$ We can factor out the $5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}-75x & =0 \\\\ 5x({x}^{2}-2x-15) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}-2x-15$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x({x}^{2}-2x-15) & =0 \\\\ 5x(x-5)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=5,$ and $x=-3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
